  "reason": "The records collectively provide several relevant ingredients: rapid cooling can kinetically retain metastable phases (R13), cooling or other processing can control dual-phase structures (R10-R16, R19), solidification chemistry can generate Laves phases and compositional partitioning (R1, R17, R20), and an antiferromagnetic constituent can reduce saturation magnetization in a ferromagnetic HEA (R8). Dual-phase structures are also repeatedly associated with interface or pinning effects, although only for mechanical properties. However, none concerns FeCoNiTi, identifies an fcc+C14 microstructure produced by water-cooled arc melting, or links that phase combination to Ti-sublattice antiferromagnetic coupling, spin canting, orbital-moment-assisted domain-wall pinning, the three magnetic transitions, or approximately 667 Oe coercivity. Thus the literature points broadly toward quench-controlled multiphase HEAs and magnetic-moment dilution, but reconstructing the specific intervention and magnetic mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
